## Authority order

1. Current explicit user direction.
2. Declared active product/operator authority and controlled external requirements.
3. Accepted ADR/design authority within scope.
4. Current architecture and operations.
5. Code/config/tests/CI/IaC/live reads as current-state evidence.
6. Draft/discovery sources.
7. History/superseded material.

## Source roles and mutation rules

| Path | Content role | Mutation rule | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `docs/product/` | approved product authority | agent-editable through reviewed changes | product requirements and capabilities |
| `docs/decisions/` | accepted decision authority | append/supersede through reviewed ADRs | durable decisions |
| {{SOURCE_PATH}} | {{CONTENT_ROLE}} | {{MUTATION_RULE}} | {{SCOPE}} |

Record every active human-authored/protected/controlled source here. Folder names do not imply authority.

## Document ownership

Product owns intended behavior; roadmap owns horizons/releases; GitHub owns actionable work/live state; change records own one change's plan/evidence; architecture owns current system/owners; operations owns procedures; design owns durable UI rules/assets when present.

## Drift prevention

Every material change declares affected owners and updates them in the same PR. Structural checks prove links/schema/routing only. Independent exact-head review compares canonical claims with real implementation/configuration/callers.
